ENTRY.text:
INTENT: Determine the identity-defining invariants of SDI Protocol across independent implementations, and reject any false need for a generic similarity percentage.
LOGIC: {'strategic_question': 'What common structural properties are identity-defining for two independent chains under SDI Protocol, and is there a generalizable conformance threshold for treating them as the same protocol?', 'sub_questions_resolved': [{'sub_question_id': 'q1', 'signals': [{'signal_id': 'sig_001', 'citation_url': 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2119/', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'RFC 2119 establishes that some specification terms mark absolute requirements.', 'ablation_outcome': 'VERDICT_FAILS'}, {'signal_id': 'sig_003', 'citation_url': 'https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_etr/001_099/021/01_60/etr_021e01p.pdf', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'ETSI conformance guidance distinguishes base requirements from profile-specific declarations.', 'ablation_outcome': 'VERDICT_FAILS'}], 'predicate': {'canonical_phrasing': 'Standards practice distinguishes absolute conformance requirements from optional or profile-scoped variation.', 'operator': 'EXISTS', 'lhs': 'MANDATORY_OPTIONAL_DISTINCTION_IN_PROTOCOL_CONFORMANCE', 'op': '==', 'rhs': 'TRUE', 'achieve': 'Y', 'framework_citation': 'RFC 2119, sig_001, and ETSI ETR 021, sig_003', 'namespace_selected': 'ietf', 'operand_class_entity': 'protocol_conformance.mandatory_optional_distinction', 'new_vocabulary_proposal': False, 'binding_status': 'BOUND', 'operand_class': 'ietf.protocol_conformance.mandatory_optional_distinction'}}, {'sub_question_id': 'q2', 'signals': [{'signal_id': 'sig_002', 'citation_url': 'https://www.sdi-protocol.org/protocol', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'The SDI specification assigns artifact structure and compile semantics to the open protocol layer itself.', 'ablation_outcome': 'VERDICT_FAILS'}], 'predicate': {'canonical_phrasing': "The named SDI chain properties belong to the protocol's constitutive structure and semantics, not merely to deployment choice.", 'operator': 'EXISTS', 'lhs': 'SDI_NAMED_PROPERTIES_ARE_PROTOCOL_INVARIANTS', 'op': '==', 'rhs': 'TRUE', 'achieve': 'Y', 'framework_citation': 'SDI Protocol specification, sig_002', 'namespace_selected': 'sdi_chain', 'operand_class_entity': 'protocol_identity.named_chain_properties_invariant_status', 'new_vocabulary_proposal': False, 'binding_status': 'BOUND', 'operand_class': 'sdi_chain.protocol_identity.named_chain_properties_invariant_status'}}, {'sub_question_id': 'q3', 'signals': [{'signal_id': 'sig_003', 'citation_url': 'https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_etr/001_099/021/01_60/etr_021e01p.pdf', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'ETSI expresses conformance through requirements and profiles rather than percentage similarity.', 'ablation_outcome': 'VERDICT_FAILS'}, {'signal_id': 'sig_001', 'citation_url': 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2119/', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'RFC 2119 supports requirement-level reasoning but supplies no universal quantitative identity threshold.', 'ablation_outcome': 'SURVIVES_VIA_OTHERS'}], 'predicate': {'canonical_phrasing': 'Retrieved conformance practice does not establish a universal quantitative threshold of structural agreement for protocol identity.', 'operator': 'EXISTS', 'lhs': 'UNIVERSAL_PERCENT_CONFORMANCE_THRESHOLD_EXISTS', 'op': '==', 'rhs': 'FALSE', 'achieve': 'Y', 'framework_citation': 'ETSI ETR 021, sig_003, with RFC 2119, sig_001', 'namespace_selected': 'ietf', 'operand_class_entity': 'protocol_conformance.universal_percent_threshold_absence', 'new_vocabulary_proposal': False, 'binding_status': 'BOUND', 'operand_class': 'ietf.protocol_conformance.universal_percent_threshold_absence'}}, {'sub_question_id': 'q4', 'signals': [{'signal_id': 'sig_004', 'citation_url': 'https://arxiv.org/html/2603.24747', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'The formal semantics paper shows protocol identity can be assessed through structural bisimilarity of agent processes.', 'ablation_outcome': 'VERDICT_FAILS'}, {'signal_id': 'sig_002', 'citation_url': 'https://www.sdi-protocol.org/protocol', 'captured_utc': '2026-08-19T18:35:50.060464+00:00', 'reasoning_contribution': 'The SDI source identifies a protocol whose primary object is structured reasoning and commit semantics.', 'ablation_outcome': 'SURVIVES_VIA_OTHERS'}], 'predicate': {'canonical_phrasing': 'Protocol identity concepts can generalize from message exchange to structured reasoning or agent process traces.', 'operator': 'EXISTS', 'lhs': 'TRACE_LEVEL_PROTOCOL_IDENTITY_GENERALIZATION', 'op': '==', 'rhs': 'TRUE', 'achieve': 'Y', 'framework_citation': 'Formal Semantics for Agentic Tool Protocols, sig_004, applied to SDI structure in sig_002', 'namespace_selected': 'arxiv', 'operand_class_entity': 'agent_protocols.trace_level_identity_generalization', 'new_vocabulary_proposal': False, 'binding_status': 'BOUND', 'operand_class': 'arxiv.agent_protocols.trace_level_identity_generalization'}}]}
JUDGMENT: {'rollup_type': 'CONJUNCTION', 'conjunction_prose': "RFC 2119 states that MUST and REQUIRED mark absolute requirements, and ETSI's conformance testing guidance states that protocol conformance is organized through base requirements, conformance statements, and profiles, therefore standards practice distinguishes identity-defining requirements from permitted variation. The SDI Protocol specification states that the open protocol defines the reasoning contract, artifact structure, and compile semantics, therefore hash-chaining integrity, gate conjunction identity, and DER-schema level commitments belong to the constitutive protocol layer. ETSI's profile and conformance material, together with RFC 2119's requirement vocabulary, states conformance by required sets rather than percentage resemblance, therefore there is no general universal numeric threshold for protocol identity. The process-calculus paper states that agent protocols can be proven structurally bisimilar under a mapping and non-equivalent where mappings are partial or lossy, therefore conformance concepts can be generalized to reasoning traces. These findings together mean two independent SDI chains count as the same protocol when they share the same constitutive contract, integrity linkage, schema and compile semantics, and required gate logic, while differences in commit authority, deployment architecture, or managed-network functions do not by themselves defeat common protocol membership.", 'state': 'HOLDS'}
OUTCOME: STATE=ANSWER_COMMITTED
GOVERNED_ANSWER: The minimum class of shared invariants for protocol identity is not surface resemblance and not a percentage of overlap. It is the set of constitutive requirements that the specification itself makes load-bearing for conformance. The retrieved standards materials support a familiar distinction: absolute requirements define membership, while optional features, declared profiles, and implementation details create permitted variation. Applied here, two independently operated reasoning chains can be treated as the same SDI Protocol only if they share the protocol-level contract that makes an SDI act what it is: governed reasoning under a pre-output contract, deterministic gate semantics at the commit boundary, structured artifact conformance, and integrity linkage to verified prior state. The retrieved SDI specification places artifact structure and compile semantics in the open protocol layer, while separating canonical commit authority and managed-network enforcement into a distinct layer. That means chain-level properties such as hash-chaining integrity, gate conjunction identity, and DER-schema conformance are identity-defining insofar as they belong to the protocol's constitutive artifact and commit semantics. By contrast, implementation venue, managed-network role, deployment topology, and similar operational choices can differ without defeating common protocol membership, so long as they do not alter those constitutive semantics. The evidence does not support any generalizable quantitative threshold like seventy percent overlap or similar. Conformance practice is expressed through satisfaction of required elements and, where relevant, explicit profiles, not by a universal similarity percentage. The formal-semantics source also supports transferring this reasoning beyond classic wire protocols: protocol sameness and difference can be judged over structured agent processes by equivalence mappings and by whether any reverse mapping is complete or lossy. So the correct third-party standard is qualitative but disciplined: same protocol if the chains share the same mandatory structural and semantic invariants, variant or merely similar if they diverge on any load-bearing invariant, even if many superficial features still match.
